+## Pitfalls
+
+1. **Fixing before reproducing**: Implementing a change without a reliable reproduction case means you cannot verify whether the fix works. If you cannot reproduce the bug, stop and investigate the environment before touching any code.
+2. **Paraphrasing the error message**: Rewording the error loses key tokens — exception class, line numbers, module paths — that pinpoint the cause. Always copy verbatim and save it: `python -m pytest -xvs 2>&1 | tee /tmp/debug.log`.
+3. **Skipping `git log` before diving into code**: A recently merged commit is the most common root cause of regressions. Run `git log --oneline -10` first — the breaking change is often immediately visible without reading any code.
+4. **Debugging in a non-isolated environment**: Running debug sessions against shared databases or live APIs corrupts shared state for other developers. Reproduce in isolation — a local virtualenv, `docker-compose up`, or a feature-branch database.
+5. **Fixing the symptom instead of the root cause**: Catching and swallowing an exception, or adding a special-case branch, leaves the underlying defect intact. The failing test you write in Phase 4 step 12 must exercise the root cause directly, not just mask its surface effect.
+6. **Not running the full test suite after the fix**: A narrowly targeted fix often introduces regressions elsewhere. Always run `python -m pytest tests/ -q` before committing — never only run the single test that was failing.
+7. **Assuming instead of measuring**: Guessing the cause and jumping to a fix without a falsifiable hypothesis wastes hours. Write the hypothesis down ("I think X because Y"), then prove or disprove it with a targeted log line or assertion before modifying any production code.
+
+## Debug Checklist
+
+- [ ] Exact error copied verbatim
+- [ ] Reproduction case in isolation
+- [ ] Recent changes reviewed (`git log -10`)
+- [ ] Hypothesis stated before looking at code
+- [ ] Fix addresses root cause (not symptom)
+- [ ] Regression test written
+- [ ] Full test suite passes
